course: dictionary
lesson: 8497
title: Sanskrit, an ancient language
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sanskrit is a very old language from India. Long ago, people used it for holy texts and for old poems and stories. Many words we use today came from it.
explain: Sanskrit is related to English, Latin and Greek. These languages belong to one family, called Indo-European, so some of their words look and sound alike. Think of them as cousins, not copies.
explain: «From Sanskrit, the ancient language of India, came "guru" (teacher), "pundit" (learned person) and "avatar" (a god coming to earth in a body, now also your character in a game).» The words in brackets are the clues. They tell you what each word means, so you do not need a dictionary for these three.
fact: Sanskrit is an ancient language of India.
fact: It was used for Hindu holy texts, such as the Vedas, and for old poems and stories.
fact: English words like «yoga», «karma» and «avatar» come from Sanskrit.
fact: Sanskrit is related to English, Latin and Greek through the Indo-European family.
fact: «Guru» means teacher, and «pundit» means learned person.
exercise: What is Sanskrit? | A kind of Scottish dinner | *An ancient language of India | A modern game console | A type of petrol
exercise: Which English word comes from Sanskrit? | *Yoga | Window | Pencil | Table
exercise: «Pundit» means what? | *A learned person | A loud shout | A clever sailor | A small bird
test: Sanskrit is related to English through which family? | Gen Alpha | Hindu | *Indo-European | Scottish
test: What does «guru» mean? | A holy river | A Hindu temple | *A teacher | A king
exam: Which sentence about Sanskrit is true? | It is a modern computer code | *It is an ancient language of India, related to English, Latin and Greek | It is spoken in every shop in London | It is a kind of avatar
